bulk_import into a cluster RT table over SQL: the loading node delivers the chunk and one BULK_IMPORT_ATTACH command attaches it on every node; the cluster files seqno becomes the seqno of that command everywhere and no staging is left.
––– block: ../base/replication/bulk-cluster-setup –––
––– input –––
source /tmp/bc.sh; load 1 t 1 3000; echo "rc $?"
––– output –––
rc 0
––– input –––
source /tmp/bc.sh; st t
––– output –––
node 1: synced count=3000 sum=4501500 last_committed=2 files_seqno=2 repl=0
node 2: synced count=3000 sum=4501500 last_committed=2 files_seqno=2 repl=0
node 3: synced count=3000 sum=4501500 last_committed=2 files_seqno=2 repl=0
––– input –––
source /tmp/bc.sh; for n in 1 2 3; do echo "node $n: $(m $n 'SELECT id,title FROM t WHERE id IN (1,1500,3000)' | sort -n | tr '\t\n' ': ') files=$(m $n "SELECT file FROM t.@files OPTION format='bulk_import'" | wc -l)"; done
––– output –––
node 1: 1:title 1 1500:title 1500 3000:title 3000  files=0
node 2: 1:title 1 1500:title 1500 3000:title 3000  files=0
node 3: 1:title 1 1500:title 1500 3000:title 3000  files=0
––– comment –––
A second load on node 3 into the non-empty table replaces the overlapping ids 2001-3000
––– input –––
source /tmp/bc.sh; load 3 t 2001 2000; echo "rc $?"; st t
––– output –––
rc 0
node 1: synced count=4000 sum=8002000 last_committed=3 files_seqno=3 repl=0
node 2: synced count=4000 sum=8002000 last_committed=3 files_seqno=3 repl=0
node 3: synced count=4000 sum=8002000 last_committed=3 files_seqno=3 repl=0
––– comment –––
Ordinary writes go on after the loads
––– input –––
source /tmp/bc.sh; m 2 "INSERT INTO v1:t (id,title,v) VALUES (5000,'plain',0)"; st t
––– output –––
node 1: synced count=4001 sum=8002000 last_committed=4 files_seqno=3 repl=0
node 2: synced count=4001 sum=8002000 last_committed=4 files_seqno=3 repl=0
node 3: synced count=4001 sum=8002000 last_committed=4 files_seqno=3 repl=0
––– comment –––
Rows without the cluster prefix are refused with the text of an ordinary INSERT (E21); a table out of the cluster refuses rows with a prefix
––– input –––
mysql -h0 -P1306 -e "INSERT INTO t (id,title,v) VALUES (9001,'plain',0)"; printf "SET bulk_import=t;\nINSERT INTO t (id,title,v) VALUES (9001,'bulk',0);\nCOMMIT;\n" | mysql -h0 -P1306; mysql -h0 -P1306 -e "CREATE TABLE l (title text, v int)"; printf "SET bulk_import=l;\nINSERT INTO v1:l (id,title,v) VALUES (1,'bulk',0);\nCOMMIT;\n" | mysql -h0 -P1306; source /tmp/bc.sh; echo "t count=$(m 1 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t') l count=$(m 1 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM l')"
––– output –––
ERROR 1064 (42000) at line 1: table 't' is a part of cluster 'v1', use 'v1:t'
ERROR 1064 (42000) at line 2: table 't' is a part of cluster 'v1', use 'v1:t'
ERROR 1064 (42000) at line 2: table 'l' is not in any cluster, use just 'l'
t count=4001 l count=0
––– comment –––
Evidence from the loading node 1 and a receiver: the chunk was prepared on two nodes and attached by the command
––– input –––
chk() { grep -qE -- "$3" /var/log/manticore-$1/searchd.log && echo "yes: $2" || echo "no: $2"; }; chk 1 'prepared on 2 nodes' "bulk_import chunk [0-9]+ of table 't': prepared on 2 nodes"; chk 1 'attached by the command' "attach command finished in .* result 1"; chk 2 'receiver attached' "bulk_import chunk [0-9]+ of table 't': attached in .* files seqno 2"; for n in 1 2 3; do grep -c 'CRASH DUMP' /var/log/manticore-$n/searchd.log; done
––– output –––
yes: prepared on 2 nodes
yes: attached by the command
yes: receiver attached
0
0
0
––– block: ../base/replication/node-starts –––
